<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1860 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Steal Away</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A spiritual made by Wallace and Minerva Willis, enslaved in the Indian Territory, and sung on tour by Fisk University's Jubilee Singers.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Wallace Willis and Minerva Willis</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Wallace Willis and Minerva Willis</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>African Americans enslaved in the Indian Territory</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Indian Territory (present-day Oklahoma), United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>before 1862</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (African American spiritual by Wallace Willis and Minerva Willis, before 1862)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Steal Away&quot;, or &quot;Steal Away to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>&quot;, is an American spiritual, best known through the variations of its chorus. It was composed some time before 1862 by Wallace Willis and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> Willis, who were enslaved in the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> by a Choctaw owner. Alexander Reid, a minister at a Choctaw boarding school, heard Willis singing his songs and wrote down their words and melodies. He sent them to the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University in Nashville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, who made them widely known on their tours of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>&quot;Steal Away&quot; is counted, with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a>&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a>&quot; and &quot;The Gospel Train&quot;, among the spirituals said to carry hidden codes: besides their faith in God, messages telling enslaved people to run away, on their own or by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. It became a standard gospel song and is in the hymnals of many Protestant churches. Michael Tippett set it in his oratorio A Child of Our Time, first performed in 1944; Pat Boone and Nat King Cole are among the many who recorded it, and in the Slovene film Valley of Peace (1956) it is sung by Jim, an American airman played by John Kitzmiller.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to steal away home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, because the singer has not long to stay here. On its face a song of faith and of longing for heaven, it has also been heard as a hidden call to slip away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The mood is quiet and urgent.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Steal away, steal away, steal away to Jesus!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Steal away, steal away home, I hain't got long to stay here</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: African American spiritual (Wallace and Minerva Willis, before 1862); the chorus as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steal_Away" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
